    {
      "id": "VER-CS-08",
      "criterion": "`version` reads every `.md` file directly inside the configured changesets directory (default `.changeset`) except `README.md`, in path order; a missing directory means no changesets, and one unparsable file aborts the run with an error naming that file.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-01",
      "criterion": "A package's severity is the highest severity among all changeset entries naming it and its inferred severity."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-02",
      "criterion": "An entry name resolves to a workspace package by prefixed id (`cargo/foo`) or bare name; a bare name that matches packages in two or more ecosystems aborts the run with an ambiguous-name error (E103).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-03",
      "criterion": "An entry naming a package not in the workspace, or an invalid name, produces an unknown-package warning, and a changeset with any such unresolved entry stays on disk unconsumed, even when its other entries resolve. An entry with severity `none` causes no bump and no changelog line, but its changeset is still consumed."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-04",
      "criterion": "Commit inference runs only for packages with `release-trigger = \"auto\"`; packages with the default `release-trigger = \"changeset\"` take severity from changesets alone. Inference reads conventional commits touching the package's directory since its last release tag (all history when untagged): a `!` marker or `BREAKING CHANGE`/`BREAKING-CHANGE` footer maps to major, `feat` to minor, `fix`/`perf`/`revert` to patch, anything else to none."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-05",
      "criterion": "`pre-major-inference` changes only inferred severity, and only for a package with a prior release tag whose current version is `0.y.z` with y >= 1: `conservative` maps major to minor, `conservative-feat` also maps minor to patch, and `off` (default) changes nothing."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-AGG-06",
      "criterion": "When no changeset is consumed, `version` warns \"No pending changesets found in workspace\" unless `--allow-empty-changesets` or `[validation] allow-empty-changesets = true` is set; with `--strict` the warning is an error and `version` exits with a failure status.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-01",
      "criterion": "When a package is bumped, each dependent's spec on it is tested against the new version. Under `[cascade] mode = \"out-of-range\"` (default) a runtime, optional, build or peer dependent is bumped only when its spec no longer covers the new version; under `mode = \"always\"` it is bumped regardless. Coverage governs only the dependent's own severity bump; whether its spec is rewritten follows VER-CAS-06."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-02",
      "criterion": "A cascaded bump has severity `bump-severity` (`patch` default, or `minor`), never lowers a severity the dependent already has, and propagates to that dependent's own dependents."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-03",
      "criterion": "With `peer-escalation = true` (default), a peer dependent whose spec no longer covers a minor or major bump of its dependency receives a major bump; a patch bump of the dependency cascades at `bump-severity`."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-04",
      "criterion": "A dev dependent is never bumped by the cascade, but its spec is still rewritten when it no longer covers the new version."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-05",
      "criterion": "Coverage: an exact spec covers only the equal version, a bare Cargo version is a caret requirement, a caret requirement never covers a prerelease, a Cargo `workspace = true` spec is tested using the root `[workspace.dependencies]` spec, and an npm `workspace:` shorthand (`workspace:`, `workspace:*`, `workspace:^` or `workspace:~`) always covers. A pnpm `workspace:` explicit range or exact version is tested using npm SemVer rules; an alias uses the range after its final `@`, and a relative path binds only to the admitted npm member at that directory. A catalog or other untestable spec is treated as covering and never rewritten; a catalog spec emits a CatalogSpecNotRewritten warning."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-06",
      "criterion": "A spec that no longer covers the new version is rewritten to it, keeping the operator and precision for Cargo and npm (`^1.2` becomes `^2.0` for 2.0.0), or to the exact version for npm when `preserve-npm-ranges = false`. A runtime, optional, build or peer dependent that is itself released in this run, for any reason, has every such spec on a bumped internal dependency raised to the dependency's new version the same way, even when the spec still covers it; a dependent that is not releasing keeps its spec unless out of range. `workspace = true`, pnpm rolling `workspace:` shorthands and catalog specs are never rewritten by either rule; an out-of-range pnpm explicit range is rewritten while retaining its `workspace:` protocol and alias. A dev dependent's spec is rewritten only when out of range. An npm range may carry a prerelease or build clause (`^2.0.0-next.0` becomes `^3.0.0`). A runtime, optional, build or peer spec that is out of range and cannot be rewritten mechanically, such as an npm range with spaces, `||`, a hyphen or a wildcard, or a Cargo range whose upper bound excludes the new version, fails the run with E319 before anything is written. A dev spec that cannot be rewritten is left unchanged with a RangeNotRoundTrippable warning."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-07",
      "criterion": "A rewrite uses the source declaration's manifest ecosystem and preserves its declared dependency key or spelling, independent of either package's display prefix. A Cargo dependency's `package` name resolves its native target while edits retain its alias; inherited aliases resolve the declaration in the Cargo workspace that governs the member. Python distribution names match using PEP 503 normalization while edits retain the spelling in the dependency declaration. A Cargo dependency inherited with `workspace = true` is rewritten in the workspace root's `[workspace.dependencies]`, not in the member manifest."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-09",
      "criterion": "A Python dependency range on a package released with its dependent is rewritten to cover the new version: `==`, `~=` and `>=` keep their operator; a two-clause `>=X,<Y` range becomes `>=NEW` with an upper bound one minor above NEW when the original spanned a single minor (`>=0.1.0,<0.2`) and the next major otherwise; and a Poetry caret or tilde becomes the equivalent PEP 440 range around NEW. A pre-release target is not written into a caret or tilde, and any other form is left unchanged with a `range-not-round-trippable` warning.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-CAS-08",
      "criterion": "The cascade aborts with E105 when it does not settle within 4 x (package count) + 1 steps, and with a grammar-mismatch error when a spec and the new version use different version grammars (SemVer vs PEP 440).",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-01",
      "criterion": "All package members of a `[[fixed-group]]` receive the group's highest severity and one shared version, computed the same grammar- and pre-release-aware way as any other bump (VER-PRE-03): the alignment base is the on-disk version of the group's released member (a member with a release tag) if the group has one, otherwise the highest on-disk version among its members, bumped by that severity."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-02",
      "criterion": "A fixed group whose released members have different on-disk versions aborts the run with E106, one whose released members use different version grammars (or whose untagged members do) aborts with E107, one whose released member has no on-disk base version aborts with E205, and one with no live member carrying a base version aborts with E206.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-06",
      "criterion": "A computed bump — for a single package or a fixed group's shared target — that would sort behind the version it was bumped from aborts the run with E207 instead of writing the regressed version to disk.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-03",
      "criterion": "When any member of a `[[linked-group]]` is bumped, every member receives the group's highest severity and all members move to the highest version any member's own bump produces (members at 1.4.0 and 2.7.3 with a minor bump both become 2.8.0)."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-04",
      "criterion": "A package claimed by two groups is rejected: E109 when the claims use different spellings of the same package, and a group-conflict error when they use the same spelling.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-GRP-05",
      "criterion": "An attached platform package, or a fixed-group member naming a platform manifest, is not bumped as a package: when its owner is bumped, the platform manifest gets the owner's new version and the owner's `optionalDependencies` entry for it is pinned to that version. A co-located platform manifest that is also one of the owner's canonical manifests is written once, as part of the owner's own bump."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-01",
      "criterion": "`callisto pre enter <tag>` writes `.changeset/pre.json` with `mode` `pre`, the tag, every package's current version under `initialVersions`, keyed by its display id (`foo`, or `cargo/foo` when another ecosystem has a `foo`) and an empty `changesets` list, then stages the file with git; under `--dry-run` it prints the content and writes nothing. For promoted packages, an older bare key is assigned to the unique historical owner at the commit that added pre.json; if no unique owner exists, version fails with E245."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-02",
      "criterion": "`pre enter` fails when the tag is empty or whitespace, or when `.changeset/pre.json` exists with mode `pre` (also under `--dry-run`); when it exists with mode `exit`, `pre enter` instead resumes the cycle under the new tag, keeping its recorded `initialVersions` and `changesets`. `callisto pre exit` sets `mode` to `exit` and fails when the file is missing or already in `exit` mode.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-03",
      "criterion": "In pre mode, a bumped package's version is its `initialVersions` entry bumped by its severity plus `-{tag}.N`, where N starts at 0 and increments while the release part is unchanged (0.1.0 with a minor changeset and tag `beta` gives 0.2.0-beta.0, then 0.2.0-beta.1); PEP 440 packages use `aN` for tags `a`/`alpha`, `bN` for `b`/`beta`, `rcN` for `rc`/`c`/`pre`/`preview` (case-insensitive), and `.devN` for any other tag."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-04",
      "criterion": "In pre mode, `version` never deletes a changeset from disk. A changeset whose id is not yet in `pre.json`'s `changesets` list counts toward severity, produces a changelog entry, and has its id added to `changesets`; a changeset whose id is already there is not pending again: a package named only by recorded changesets is not bumped, so a rerun with nothing new changes nothing, and a package named by a new changeset still counts the recorded ones toward its severity. The `allow-empty-changesets`/`--strict` handling from VER-AGG-06 triggers when no changeset in pre mode is new."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-05",
      "criterion": "After `pre exit`, the next `version` moves every package at a prerelease version to its stable release (0.2.0-beta.1 becomes 0.2.0), consumes and deletes the pending changesets, and deletes `pre.json`."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-06",
      "criterion": "A malformed `.changeset/pre.json` (invalid JSON, a missing `mode`, `tag`, `initialVersions` or `changesets` field, a mode other than `pre` or `exit`, or an unparsable version) makes `version` fail with E114 and write nothing; an unreadable file fails with E115.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-07",
      "criterion": "`callisto snapshot --tag <tag>` sets every workspace package, and every attached platform package and fixed-group platform manifest, to a snapshot version built from the same tag and sha7 (the first 7 characters of HEAD) in the package's own grammar: `0.0.0-{tag}-{sha7}` for a SemVer package, `0.0.0.dev0+{tag}.{sha7}` for a Pep440 package; it fails if HEAD cannot be resolved. A snapshot version is never tagged or published: `release` refuses it with E169."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-08",
      "criterion": "A snapshot writes manifests and refreshes each active ecosystem's existing lockfile, unconditionally (there is no `--no-refresh-lockfiles` for `snapshot`), so a subsequent locked install resolves against the snapshot version; it ignores and keeps pending changesets, writes no changelog or `pre.json`, and stages nothing in git; under `--dry-run` it writes nothing."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-09",
      "criterion": "For every workspace dependency whose current spec does not cover the snapshot version, `snapshot` rewrites that spec using the source declaration's manifest ecosystem and declared key or spelling, including Cargo aliases. Inherited Cargo declarations are rewritten in the root manifest of the Cargo workspace that governs the member rather than the member manifest. A spec that already covers the snapshot version is left unchanged."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-PRE-10",
      "criterion": "Every `pre.json` initial version is validated using the owning package's native grammar after historical and promoted identity resolution; the original JSON string is validated, not a spelling normalized by a cross-ecosystem fallback. A malformed owned baseline fails with E114 before version, changelog, pre-state or changeset writes during `version`, `pre exit` or resuming with `pre enter`."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-01",
      "criterion": "`version` writes the new version to every canonical manifest of a bumped package, so both manifests of a dual-manifest package move together."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-02",
      "criterion": "Before writing a package or platform manifest, apply reads its current version: equal to the planned `from` it is written, equal to the target it is left unchanged, so applying the same plan again is safe."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-03",
      "criterion": "A manifest whose current version is neither the planned `from` nor the target aborts apply with E117 naming the path, the found version and both expected versions.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-04",
      "criterion": "A manifest that receives a version bump and dependency rewrites in the same run is read once and written once with all changes, and a manifest already at its target with no rewrites is not rewritten. An owner's `optionalDependencies` pins, and edits to a workspace root `Cargo.toml` that is also a package manifest, are written separately."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-05",
      "criterion": "Files are written one at a time, each by atomic replacement, so an interrupted run never leaves a partially written file; a failing write stops the run and leaves earlier files in place. A write also fails when the directory holding the new file cannot be synced, unless the filesystem cannot sync a directory."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-17",
      "criterion": "Before the first write, every file the plan edits, creates or deletes is checked: a manifest must exist as a readable regular file, no path may be a symbolic link or sit beneath one (Git cannot stage it), no path may leave the workspace, the directory of each written file (or its nearest existing parent) must take a new file, and a file the plan stages must not be ignored by a .gitignore unless Git already tracks it. Checking creates no file or directory. Any failure stops the run with E314 naming every such path, and nothing has been written, staged or deleted. A lockfile that a .gitignore ignores is still refreshed on disk but is not staged. The checks cover only what can be known beforehand: a lockfile refresh that fails (E208) or a Git command that fails while staging can still stop the run after the writes.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-READ-01",
      "criterion": "Only a missing file is treated as absent when reading `callisto.toml`, `.changeset/pre.json`, a changelog, or the changeset directory. A file that cannot be read or inspected (a permission error, a directory, invalid UTF-8) fails the command naming the path; a dangling link or an entry removed during listing holds no changeset.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-READ-02",
      "criterion": "Only \"not found\" is treated as absent when callisto asks Git about a tag, ref, path or file content: a tag list, a ref that does not resolve, a path missing at a revision, a search with no match, and a repository with no commits yet each answer \"none\". Any other Git failure (not a repository, a broken repository, a revision that cannot be searched) fails the command naming the Git command and its error. A tag that is listed but does not resolve to a commit fails `status` and `version` instead of widening commit inference to the whole history.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-READ-04",
      "criterion": "Planning fails with E315 in a shallow clone when any package infers its bump from commits (`release-trigger = \"auto\"`), because the commit window would be shorter than the real one. Packages that take their bump from changesets alone are unaffected.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-READ-03",
      "criterion": "`status` reports a tag that matches a package's tag template but is not a valid version as one warning naming the tag, however many packages share the template.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-18",
      "criterion": "A file callisto rewrites keeps its permission bits, and a file it creates gets the mode any new file gets under the process umask."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-06",
      "criterion": "For each bumped package, apply prepends a `## {version}` section under the `# {package}` heading of its changelog (default `CHANGELOG.md` in the package directory, overridable with `changelog`), creating the file if missing and keeping CRLF line endings; a changelog that already has that heading is left unchanged."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-07",
      "criterion": "A changelog section lists each changeset summary as a bullet (continuation lines indented), an inferred bump as a commit subject with its short sha, a group bump as \"Released together with the `{group}` {fixed|linked} group.\", a new fixed-group member as \"Joined the `{group}` group at this version.\", a peer escalation as \"Peer dependency `{dep}` requires `{version}`\", and cascaded bumps as \"`{dep}` → `{version}`\" lines under a \"Dependency updates\" bullet."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-08",
      "criterion": "Outside pre mode, `version` refreshes affected lockfiles and stages written manifests, changelogs, `pre.json` changes and existing lockfiles before consuming release inputs. It stages consumed changeset and `pre.json` deletions via `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch` before unlinking those files from disk. If refresh, generated-state staging or input-deletion staging fails, changesets and pre-state deletion inputs remain on disk for retry."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-09",
      "criterion": "`version --dry-run` prints the computed plan (text output starts with `[DRY-RUN]`) and writes, deletes and stages nothing."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-10",
      "criterion": "`version --emit-decision <path>` writes the release decision JSON to `<path>` relative to the workspace root after the plan has been applied successfully: `schemaVersion` 2, a digest, and one entry per canonical manifest of each bumped package (ecosystem-qualified name, target version, reasons) sorted by package. Reason `kind` is `changeset`, `inference`, `fixedGroup` or `linkedGroup` (with `groupId`), `cascade` (with source package and edge kind, `peer` for peer escalation) or `preReleasePolicy` (with the prerelease tag)."
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-11",
      "criterion": "`--emit-decision` with `--dry-run` is rejected before any work, and a plan with no bumps, or whose group members have different target versions, fails to produce a decision before any file is written.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-12",
      "criterion": "A diagnostic escalated to an error (for example `--strict` on the empty-changeset warning) is evaluated right after planning: `version` writes nothing -- not the manifests and changelog, not `pre.json`, not the `--emit-decision` file -- and exits with a failure status, instead of applying the plan and only then failing.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "VER-APPLY-13",
